{quote}After investigation it was discovered, that your data loader doesn’t sort all data for IMDS.{quote}

Tests on pre-live have shown that there is no issue with the sorting at the moment. While in theory it is possible that database returns rows in different order (because some queries don’t have {{ORDER BY}} clause, thus the order is not guaranteed), in practice TiDB uses TiKV as a storage engine for our tables, which guarantees that the rows will be ordered by their primary keys during full table scans.

{quote}If *TiKV* is used as the storage engine, the rows are retrieved in the order of their *row IDs* (or keys). Row IDs are stored lexicographically as keys in TiKV, which means rows will be scanned in the ascending order of these row keys. TiDB assigns an internal row ID if no primary key or unique index is defined.{quote}

So, to keep things simple I’d avoid explicit sorting until we face a real life scenario when the result order fluctuates.

